{Game started 1.e3... later this position MAY have happened... "At the evening
sitting a large crowd watched the continuation of the game between Blackburne
and Michell, and were rewarded by some exciting chess. Blackburne broke up the
position, but not at all favourably to himself, as he ran short of time to do
justice to the complications. When the rooks and bishops were exchanged
Michell was left with queen and four pawns against Blackburneâ€™s queen and
three in the following position:-  White (Blackburne)- K on K Kt 2, Q on K 7,
pawns on K R 2, K Kt 3, K B 4.  Black (Michell)-K on K R 3. Q on Q R 4, pawns
on K R 2, K Kt 3, K R B 4 [sic], Q 4.  Here Blackburne played:- 1 K to R 3, Q
to Rl. 2 K to R 4, Q to K Kt 1, 3 P to K R 3, but Michell would not fall into
the trap, and played his queen away, whereupon a draw was the only result
possible, as White has perpetual check." (Source: Yorkshire Post and Leeds
Intelligencer - Wednesday 21 August 1907) ... but Blackburne missed a win (JS).
..} 1. Kh3 ({Under time pressure, Blackburne misses} 1. Qf8+ $1 Kh5 2. Qg7 h6
3. Qe7 Qa2+ 4. Kh3 {and mate follows.}) 1... Qa8 2. Kh4 Qg8 3. h3 (3. h3 {
We're not told Black's next move ("... played his queen away") but, to draw by
force, he has to find} Qb8 $1 {when} 4. g4 fxg4 {and White has to take the
draw by perpetual check.}) 1/2-1/2

