[EventType "tourn"]
[EventRounds "11"]
[EventCountry "GBR"]

{The Field, 31 August: "It was an important game for Blackburne, as a win
meant second prize absolutely, a draw a tie with three others, and a loss
nothing!" Mackenzie stood better throughout, thanks to the active pieces and
open lines. He missed numerous opportunities to win the endgame.} 1. e4 e5 2.
Nf3 Nf6 3. Nc3 Nc6 4. Bb5 Bb4 5. O-O d6 6. Nd5 Ba5 7. d4 a6 8. Nxf6+ gxf6 9.
Bxc6+ bxc6 10. dxe5 fxe5 11. Bg5 Qd7 12. Nh4 Qg4 13. Qxg4 Bxg4 14. c3 Rb8 15.
b4 Bb6 16. Rab1 Kd7 17. Rb2 Rhg8 18. Bc1 Be6 19. Re1 Rg4 20. g3 Rbg8 21. Ng2 h5
22. Kh1 f6 23. f3 R4g7 24. Be3 d5 25. Rd2 Kc8 26. Bxb6 cxb6 27. Nh4 Kc7 28. Kg1
Rd7 29. Red1 Rgd8 30. a3 d4 31. cxd4 Rxd4 32. Rxd4 Rxd4 33. Ra1 c5 34. bxc5
bxc5 35. Nf5 Rd2 36. Rc1 Kc6 37. Rc3 c4 38. g4 hxg4 39. fxg4 Kc5 40. h4 Rd3 41.
Rxd3 cxd3 42. Kf2 Kc4 43. Ne3+ Kd4 44. Kf3 Bg8 $4 {TH: Mackenzie could easily
win by 44...d2! 45 Nd1 (45 Ke2 d1Q+! 46 Nxd1 Bxg4+ etc.) 45...Kd3 and if 46
Nf2+ Kc2-+} 45. Nf1 Bh7 46. Nd2 a5 47. h5 a4 48. h6 Bg6 49. Kf2 {Birmingham
Post: "In embarking on the variation 43...Kd4, Black overlooked, until too
late, that White could play his K away from the P - a characteristically wily
stroke on Blackburne's part, which saves the game."} Bh7 50. Kf3 Bg6 51. Kf2 {
Source of score: Birmingham Daily Post, 27 August 1907, page 8. Supplied to
Britbase by Tim Harding.} (51. Kf2 {Drawn by repetition of moves, for if} Bxe4
$4 52. Nxe4 Kxe4 53. Ke1 {it is White who wins.}) 1/2-1/2

