1. e4 c5 2. Bc4 Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. d3 d6 5. Be3 a6 6. a4 g6 7. Nd5 Bg7 8. c3 O-O
9. Ne2 Ng4 10. Ng3 Nxe3 11. fxe3 e6 12. Nf4 Bd7 13. h4 Na5 14. Ba2 e5 15. Nd5
Bh6 16. h5 Kg7 17. hxg6 fxg6 18. Rc1 Qg5 19. Ne2 Qxg2 20. Rg1 Qh3 21. Kd2 Rf2
22. b4 Nc6 23. Rh1 Qf3 24. Re1 Bg4 25. bxc5 dxc5 26. Rb1 Rb8 27. Bc4 Bg5 28.
Rb3 Na5 29. Rb6 Nxc4+ 30. dxc4 Qxe4 31. Qb3 Bxe3+ 32. Nxe3 Rd8+ 33. Nd5 Bxe2
34. Rxb7+ Kh6 35. Kc1 Rf1 36. Rxf1 Bxf1 37. Rxh7+ Kg5 38. Rh5+ gxh5 {'Resigned
by White at 55th move.' Source: Cheltenham Examiner, 11 Sept 1907. The
columnist adds in notes after Black's 38th move: 'Miss Gooding overlooked this
Black pawn, which was obscured by his King. The play at this point was too
quick . Of course if K x R white would win. Q - Qsq (instead of R -R5) with
the idea of Q -Q 2 ch, would have given White good winning chances, although a
Pawn behind.' [N.B. If 38 Qd1, Black still seems to win by 38...Qe2. White
would have had a greater chance of a draw by 38 Qb7, though Black could have
retained a definite advantage.] (Brian Denman)} 0-1

